# ==========================================================================
#  O51  What follows from what you said
# ==========================================================================
#
#  DECIDES
#    An inference is a statement true in every interpretation that
#    satisfies the ontology; a reasoner finds them; adding axioms never
#    removes one; and 'not said' is not 'false'.
#
#  WHY
#    Every settlement is a place. Wigtown is a settlement. So Wigtown is a
#    place. Nobody wrote the third sentence and it is true anyway, because
#    there is no way for the first two to hold and the third to fail. That
#    is entailment, and it is the whole of what a reasoner does: find the
#    statements that hold in every interpretation the axioms allow. The set
#    reading makes it plain. S is inside P; w is in S; so w is in P. There
#    is nothing to decide.
#
#    Three things can follow. A new triple, which a materialising reasoner
#    writes into the graph, as the editor's Show Facts does. A
#    classification, that one class is inside another though nobody said
#    so. And a contradiction, that no interpretation exists at all. RDFS
#    can give the first; OWL 2 gives all three.
#
#    Two properties of the logic decide how it feels to use. It is
#    monotonic: adding an axiom can add conclusions and never remove one,
#    so a conclusion drawn today stays drawn. Monotonicity is what makes
#    materialisation safe, and it is why a SHACL rule with a negation in
#    it, which the SHACL course's s52 shows going stale, is a different
#    kind of thing. And it is open-world: a statement not in the graph is
#    not false, it is unknown, so absence entails nothing. The reasoner
#    will never tell you that a shop has no website. A query will, and the
#    difference is the subject of the next lesson.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#       axioms                                     what follows
#
#       bs:Settlement rdfs:subClassOf bs:Place     w in S, S inside P  =>  w in P
#       bt:place-wigtown a bs:Settlement            a new triple:      bt:place-wigtown a bs:Place
#
#       three kinds of consequence:  a triple  |  a classification  |  a contradiction
#       monotonic:  more axioms, never fewer conclusions
#       open world: not said is not false; absence entails nothing
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - Entailment is what holds in every interpretation the axioms allow.
#      A reasoner finds it; a query finds what is written.
#    - Monotonic: nothing you add takes a conclusion away. That is why
#      materialising is safe and why rules with negation are not the same
#      thing.
#    - Open world: the reasoner never concludes from absence. For 'has no
#      website', ask a query.
#
#  CORNER    referent
#  RUNS IN   the browser
#  OPEN IN THE EDITOR
#            https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FOntology_Course%2Fmain%2Flessons%2F08-reasoning%2Fo51-what-follows-from-what-you-said.ttl
#  SOURCES   OWL 2 Direct Semantics 2.2 Interpretations
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-direct-semantics/#Interpretations
#            OWL 2 Primer 2.2 Modeling Knowledge: Basic Notions (the open world, no unique names)
#            https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-primer/#Modeling_Knowledge:_Basic_Notions
#  MEASURED  parses, 12 triples; 3 classes, 1 datatype property, 2 individuals; OWL 2 DL in profile; HermiT: consistent, 1 inferred; RL closure +1; 'What the reasoner cannot say' 1 row
# ==========================================================================

@prefix bt:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/> .
@prefix bs:   <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix skos: <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

skos:prefLabel      a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:definition     a owl:AnnotationProperty .
skos:editorialNote  a owl:AnnotationProperty .

bs:Place       a owl:Class ; skos:prefLabel "Place"@en .
bs:Settlement  a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf bs:Place ; skos:prefLabel "Settlement"@en .
bs:Bookshop    a owl:Class .
bs:website     a owl:DatatypeProperty .

bt:place-wigtown  a bs:Settlement .
bt:shop-marginalia a bs:Bookshop .     # no website recorded: the reasoner concludes nothing


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AFTERWARDS.  Queries to run in the SPARQL panel, on this file (press
#  Add Prefixes first) or on the data file it names.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#  -- What the reasoner cannot say: shops with no website  (on the model)
#  PREFIX bs: <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
#  SELECT ?shop WHERE { ?shop a bs:Bookshop . FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?shop bs:website ?w } }
